From 1b7f1b02807576e73e8a58c292456c5213409e8b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Michael C. Ferguson" <michael.christopher.ferguson@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 19:56:04 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 7/8] usb: dwc2: hcd_ddma: keep interrupt descriptor lists in
 coherent memory

Descriptor lists are kmem cache objects with a streaming mapping. Each
descriptor is 8 bytes, so one 32-byte cache line on ARMv7 holds four
of them, and the driver does cache maintenance per descriptor while
the core writes back the status word of every descriptor it retires.

That is only safe while the CPU never touches a line the channel is
still using. Once software writes a descriptor next to one the core is
processing, the clean for the new descriptor can write the CPU's stale
copy of its neighbour back over the core's write-back, and the
invalidate done before reading a completion (dma_sync_single_for_cpu()
with DMA_FROM_DEVICE) can discard what software just wrote to the line.

With dma_desc_int_chain, full- and low-speed interrupt endpoints are
about to keep a channel running while new descriptors are added. For
those, allocate the lists from a dma_pool in coherent memory, 512 bytes
aligned to 512 as the core requires, and skip the per-descriptor and
whole-list syncs for such lists. The pool is only created when the
parameter is set, so every other configuration, high-speed interrupt
endpoints included, keeps the kmem cache and its streaming mapping.
The pool is device managed and optional: if it cannot be created, the
lists stay in the kmem cache and the endpoint is not chained.

Coherent memory is mapped Normal non-cacheable on ARM, so ordering is
still needed. The CHENA write that starts a list goes through writel(),
which orders the descriptor writes before it; descriptors added to a
running list need dma_wmb() before the word that hands them over.

Isochronous lists share the hazard in principle, since their
completion path also rewrites descriptors of a running channel under a
streaming mapping. They are left alone here.

Signed-off-by: Michael C. Ferguson <michael.christopher.ferguson@gmail.com>
